0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.3",{"topic":{"id":"4.3","title":"Installation and Configuration of DEFT","minutes":14,"summary":"Prepare an isolated, documented environment with verified installation media, controlled storage, and a repeatable test plan.","sections":[{"title":"Plan before installing","body":["Choose an archived release only when the exercise requires it, obtain installation media through a trustworthy source, and verify its published checksum or signature where available. Record the source and verification result. Old installation media may contain vulnerable software; isolate it from untrusted networks and do not use it as an everyday internet workstation.","Allocate CPU, memory, and separate virtual disks for the operating system, read-only evidence, and outputs. Document the hypervisor, guest architecture, network mode, and snapshot state. A VM snapshot is useful for resetting a classroom tool environment, but it is not a substitute for preserving the original evidence exhibit."]},{"title":"Configure and test the environment","body":["After installation, record the release, tool versions, time settings, storage mappings, and permissions. Confirm that the evidence input is read-only through the actual configuration and that output is written to a separate location. Avoid shared clipboards, automatic mounting, and accidental drag-and-drop into the evidence directory.","Run a known synthetic test image through the intended workflow. Confirm that the tool can read the format, that expected artifacts are recovered, and that the source remains unchanged. Record errors and unsupported features. Validation should cover the specific task rather than relying on a general claim that the distribution is “forensic”."]},{"title":"Prepare a repeatable lab record","body":["Create a lab template with environment details, input identifiers and hashes, procedure, output identifiers and hashes, observations, and limitations. Use snapshots to return to a known state between exercises, but keep experiment outputs outside the disposable system disk. Explain which settings are necessary to reproduce the result.","Do not install random packages during an examination without recording the change. Dependencies and updates can alter behavior. If a tool fails, preserve the error and choose a documented alternative rather than repeatedly modifying the source. The browser labs here provide the safe conceptual sequence; they do not claim to install or emulate an entire Linux distribution."]}],"practice":{"title":"Write the VM preparation checklist","scenario":"You are preparing an archived forensic distribution for a synthetic filesystem exercise.","steps":["Record the media source and verification result.","Separate the OS, evidence, and output storage.","Set an isolated network mode and document time settings.","Run a known test image and save the environment configuration."],"debrief":"A reproducible setup makes tool behavior easier to evaluate and prevents an avoidable mix-up between evidence and experiment output."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain plan before installing and apply it to an investigation.","Explain configure and test the environment and apply it to an investigation.","Explain prepare a repeatable lab record and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.3","prompt":"Where should tool output be written in a controlled forensic lab?","options":["Into the original evidence file.","To a separate, documented output location.","Over the source directory entry.","To an unknown public upload service."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Separating inputs from outputs protects the original and makes transformations traceable.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
